00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.050
Thank you Laura.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.330
- Thanks Josh for that introduction.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.800
Thanks to Yale for having me,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.350
and thanks to everyone for coming today.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.140
I'm really excited to be here
to talk about this project,

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.980
which has been a joint effort
with Eitan Hersh at Tufts.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.270
Broadly speaking,

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.990
this project is a series of
survey questions and experiments

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.860
that we designed to examine
antisemitic attitudes

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.780
among American adults.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.880
I think we originally
envisioned this project

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.790
being one paper, but it
ended up turning into three,

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.730
because we realized we
really wanted enough space

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.550
to be able to discuss
the topic and our results

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:09.930
with the level of nuance

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.750
that I think we can all agree it deserves.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.550
Three papers is obviously way too much

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.220
to present fully in the next 45 minutes.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.830
So I'm gonna present
some of the core findings

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.360
from one of the papers, which
was recently published in PRQ,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.013
for the political scientists.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.960
And this one focuses on
ideological and age dimensions

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.960
of antisemitic attitudes in the US.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.820
So I'm gonna talk through our theory,

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.980
our hypotheses, and our findings.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.690
And end briefly by highlighting our work

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.130
in the other two papers.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.410
Of course, I'm happy to
talk about those papers,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.930
or more detailed findings
from this one later

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.553
or offline with anyone.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.230
So, why did we decide to do this project?

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.480
Well, starting about five years ago,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.660
we've seen a series of
high-profile antisemitic incidents

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:58.770
in America.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.020
We've seen the Tree of Life shooting,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.270
the Charlottesville Unite the Right March,

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:04.740
but also more common incidents

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.540
like the defacement of synagogues

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.770
and harassment of Jewish
Americans, both violently and non.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.800
And all of this has,
perhaps unsurprisingly,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.600
been paralleled by a sharp increase

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.750
in perceived concern about antisemitism.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:19.920
As of last year,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.900
62% of all Americans and
88% of Jewish Americans

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.870
now consider antisemitism a problem.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.960
One in four Jews surveyed by the AJC,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.640
or the American Jewish Committee,

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.250
said that they had personally
experienced antisemitism

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.243
in the past year.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.120
So there's certainly a recent salience

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.700
to antisemitism in American politics

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:42.630
and a sense that it's something

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.730
worth looking at empirically.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.050
But we also noticed something else

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.650
that made this a
compelling topic, which is,

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.200
that there's a lot of focus in news media,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.700
and popular commentators,

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.490
not just on this rise of antisemitism,

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.030
but specifically on how
antisemitism seems to be popping up

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.000
on both the far left and the far right.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.850
And I really mean there's a lot of focus,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.340
really quite a lot of focus

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.130
about the relationship
between extreme ideology

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.393
and antisemitism.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.530
Now this makes sense,
because many of the recent

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.660
high-profile antisemitic incidents,

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.310
like the ones I just mentioned,

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.440
have been associated with either far left

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.833
or far right politics,
not just one or the other.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.600
But a prejudice rising on
both ideological extremes,

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.340
yet not in the center,

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.470
is a fairly unique and interesting pattern

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.530
that we don't see very
much in American politics.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.950
So naturally, being the
political scientists that we are,

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.540
we decided to focus this project

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.610
on getting a clearer
picture of the pattern,

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.400
and whether it exists in
mass public attitudes,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.863
instead of just as these anecdotes.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:52.950
Now we're calling this pattern

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.200
the horseshoe pattern of antisemitism,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.510
because it mirrors a
broader theory of ideology

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.640
known as horseshoe theory.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.320
And horseshoe theory is the idea

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.750
that ideology isn't a straight line

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.820
running from far left to far right,

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.560
as we would usually think about it,

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.380
but instead it's bent like a horseshoe.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.120
So functionally what this means,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.580
is we would have the center
at the top of the horseshoe,

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.730
and bent down, so that the
far left and the far right

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.370
are actually much closer to each other

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:21.813
than they are to the center.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.113
Now horseshoe theory

00:05:24.113 --> 00:05:27.360
became somewhat well
known in American politics

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.460
around the 2016 election,

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.950
with the simultaneous
rise of the Bernie left

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.720
and the Trump right,

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.390
which arguably both share a populist base

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:39.090
and frustration with
the prior ruling elites

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.293
in both parties.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:42.780
It hasn't really gained traction

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.960
as a broad theory to
explain American politics,

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.310
but it has endured in discussions
of American antisemitism,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:51.900
Two recent popular books,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.990
one by Bari Weiss who used to
work for the New York Times,

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:57.930
and one by Deborah Lipstadt,
a well-known historian

00:05:57.930 --> 00:05:59.160
who's now actually leading

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.610
the Biden administration's
work on antisemitism,

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.950
do a great job of fully
laying out the case

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.623
for why this horseshoe
pattern might have arisen.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.590
And why might that be?

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.710
Well, extreme antisemitism on both sides

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:15.660
could have a shared cause.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.900
If burgeoning populism
among these extremes

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:22.320
leads to a frustration with
the capitalist status quo,

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.090
this could feed into old stereotypes

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.460
about Jews as shadowy overlords

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.830
who control government and finance,

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.360
which could then lead to an increase

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.903
in antisemitic attitudes on
both ideological extremes.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.380
But it could also be
for independent reasons

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.663
that happen to both be occurring now.

00:06:42.823 --> 00:06:45.810
Growing white nationalist
sentiment on the far right

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.510
might lead directly to
antisemitic attitudes,

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.030
with Jews being seen as a shady group

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:53.220
either pretending to be white,

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.790
or siding with the non-white
population politically,

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.743
given how liberal American Jews are.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:03.090
Antisemitic sentiment on the
far left, on the other hand,

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.730
may have spilled over from
their views on Israel,

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:06.720
as the political left

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.580
has steadily become more supportive

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:12.990
of the Palestinian cause, and
more critical towards Israel.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:16.650
We've seen progressive politicians
draw direct comparisons

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.480
between Black Lives Matter,

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.240
a cause that obviously holds
a lot of leftist support,

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.370
with the Palestinian cause.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.400
So if the far right has begun
to view Israel negatively,

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.300
and as the clear oppressor
in the Palestinian conflict,

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.190
those negative views may spill over

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:33.843
towards Jews more broadly.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.570
So we designed this project

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.250
primarily to examine whether or not

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.170
this horseshoe pattern of antisemitism

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.230
exists for either of the above reasons.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:44.580
But there's one important wrinkle

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.710
in the horseshoe pattern of antisemitism

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.933
that we really wanted to focus on as well.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.200
And that's what's going
on with young adults,

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.660
in terms of antisemitism.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:55.560
So there's two reasons

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:58.200
why looking at young adults'
antisemitic attitudes here

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.610
is interesting.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.340
The first is that we know from prior work

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:04.710
that young adults are
generally more tolerant,

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:06.060
less prejudicial,

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:09.570
hold more cosmopolitan
attitudes than older adults do.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.640
But a number of prior studies have found

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.910
that they actually tend to
have higher rates specifically

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:16.353
of antisemitism.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.110
It's been theorized that this may be

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:22.200
to the declining salience of the Holocaust

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.930
as we move further away from World War II,

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.580
or to the increased salience

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.650
of the Israel/Palestine conflict,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.660
particularly given the
more liberal leanings

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:32.010
of young adults.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.160
So we're interested to
see if this is still true.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.050
But we're also interested

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:39.630
to see whether there was
an ideological dimension

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.960
specific to young adult antisemitism.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.840
And that's because there are
two ideological movements

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:48.540
that may make the horseshoe
pattern more extreme,

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:51.330
specifically among young adults.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.130
On the right,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:56.250
we've seen the growth of the
white nationalist alt-right,

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.410
which has occurred primarily online,

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:00.180
and thus has gained a large following

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:01.863
specifically among young men.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:03.690
On the left,

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:06.750
we've seen a huge burst in
pro-Palestinian activism

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.780
on college campuses and
in social justice circles,

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.540
which are heavily
populated by young adults.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:13.860
Now both of these movements

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.490
may really increase the
pathways to extreme antisemitism

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.590
I described on the previous slide,

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:21.990
and thus could accentuate
the horseshoe pattern.

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.370
So we focused on young adults

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:26.343
to see if that was indeed the case.

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:31.650
So we wanted to examine mass
attitudes among young adults

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.600
and the ideological extremes.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.340
So what did we do?

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:37.380
Of course, we ran an original survey.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.740
It was fielded by YouGov
right after the 2020 election.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.830
And we have this big
2500-person oversample

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.950
of younger adults, in order
to really be able to examine

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:48.600
the rise in antisemitism

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.420
that might be most prominent
among this age group.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.820
And we also have a 1000-person sample

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:55.620
of the full adult population.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.280
So that gives us a 3500
person sample in total,

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.703
which we can and will
break into smaller groups.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:06.390
Before I get into the meat of the survey,

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:09.480
here's the distribution of
the respondents ideologically.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:10.890
Most of our analysis

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:13.950
is using a standard
seven-point ideology scale,

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:16.980
going from very liberal
to very conservative.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:19.650
But we did also duplicate the analysis

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:21.960
using an alternative measure of ideology.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:24.060
So we explicitly asked respondents

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:27.210
if they identify as more extreme labels,

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:29.610
like alt right, or leftists.

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:33.330
And rerunning all of the
analysis with those labels,

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.313
we get essentially the same results.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.880
So for simplicity and
ease of understanding,

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:40.530
I'm gonna present everything here

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.690
using this seven-point ideology scale,

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.790
but just know that this does hold up,

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:48.630
even if we just look at
more extreme identifiers.

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:51.180
So you can see the distribution here.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:54.240
We do have more liberal young adults

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:55.440
than we do older adults,

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.000
and we also have more liberal young adults

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.090
than we do conservative young adults.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.090
But 30% of our young sample
is still conservative,

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:04.470
so that's fairly good,

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.240
we have a fairly good distribution here,

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.390
and enough to really be able
to analyze these buckets.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:10.830
Our sample of older adults

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:15.243
is fairly well distributed, as well.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.560
So before I show you the questions we use

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.470
to measure antisemitism, I wanna pause,

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:24.390
and note that there have
been decades of debate

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:27.660
about how exactly to define antisemitism,

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:30.030
or what counts as antisemitism,

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:32.853
and it certainly still
remains a hot topic.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:36.210
So here, we're primarily focusing

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:40.020
on attitudes that respondents
hold towards American Jews.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.470
Do they think of Jews in
stereotypical or demonizing ways,

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.270
or do they treat Jews differently

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:48.330
from how they treat other minority groups?

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:51.030
And we decided to measure
this form of antisemitism

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:52.293
in two different ways.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.530
We measure overt antisemitism

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:59.010
via direct agreement with
specific claims about Jews.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:02.130
And then we also measure
more subtle antisemitism

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:03.600
through a series of experiments

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:06.270
about whether Jews are
held to higher standards

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:08.340
than other groups of Americans.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.560
Now, we do this for two reasons.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:13.680
The first is to feel more
confident in our results

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:16.650
by triangulating across the
strengths and weaknesses

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:18.813
of these two ways to measure prejudice.

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:23.700
The second reason is to
respond to another key aspect

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:28.380
of the popular discussion
around ideological antisemitism,

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.240
which is this repeated idea

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:33.630
that left wing antisemitism
is more subtle,

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:37.110
or that it's more insidious
and gets glossed over,

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:38.823
or that it goes under the radar.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:42.390
And that this has really allowed the left

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:44.640
to essentially hide its antisemitism,

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.213
and avoid addressing it as
sort of real antisemitism.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:51.120
So in order to try and measure

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:54.090
what these commentators argue
are somewhat hidden views,

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:55.650
we also really wanted to include

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.750
these less overt survey experiments,

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:01.800
to potentially draw out these
hidden yet honest views,

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:02.913
if they exist.

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.970
So I'm gonna start with our
overt antisemitism battery,

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:11.010
which consists of three questions

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:13.567
with different antisemitic stereotypes.

00:13:13.567 --> 00:13:17.017
"Jews are more loyal to
Israel than to America,

00:13:17.017 --> 00:13:19.807
"It's appropriate to
boycott Jewish businesses

00:13:19.807 --> 00:13:22.230
"as a form of opposition to Israel,"

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:25.080
and that "Jews in the United
States have too much power."

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:28.020
Now these are questions
that have been asked before

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:32.310
by organizations and researchers
to measure antisemitism,

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:34.080
but as an additional check,

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:37.110
we also took any respondent
who said they were Jewish,

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.400
and instead of asking them

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:40.320
if they agreed with these statements,

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:41.250
although the results there

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:42.870
might be a little bit interesting,

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:46.230
we instead ask them if they
found them antisemitic.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:47.400
And across the board,

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:50.400
at least 75% of Jewish respondents agreed

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.650
that each statement was antisemitic.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:56.610
We see agreement as high as
90% for the boycott measure.

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.230
So that's another indication

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.660
that these are probably
reasonable questions

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.253
to gauge antisemitism.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:06.960
And now we paired this
overt antisemitism battery

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.300
with a priming experiment.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:11.400
Half of our respondents were first primed

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.400
with the following quote,
which is a true finding,

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:17.610
explaining that Gallup
found that 95% of Jews

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:20.220
have favorable views of Israel.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:22.350
And we did this to see
if we could draw out

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:25.050
any of this potentially
masked antisemitism,

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:28.380
since perhaps perhaps priming respondents

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:31.710
who are already critical
of Israel with information

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:34.500
that Jews are an out group
to them on this issue

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.900
would make them respond more negatively,

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:40.800
and perhaps more honestly,
about their views of Jews.

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:42.720
Respondents may feel more okay

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.820
responding with their true beliefs,

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.100
if it could be viewed
as a criticism of Israel

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.983
instead of just
antisemitism, in other words.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:54.390
So we had a few different hypotheses.

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:57.900
First, we expected to find
that antisemitic attitudes

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:00.780
would be higher among
the ideological extremes.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:04.170
In other words, giving us
this horseshoe-shaped pattern.

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:06.150
We expected to find that younger adults

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:09.660
would in general be more
antisemitic than older adults.

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:12.540
But we also expected that
the horseshoe pattern

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:15.510
would be more extreme
among younger adults.

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:16.830
In other words,

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:19.980
we expected that most of
the increase in antisemitism

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:21.480
among younger adults

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:24.750
would be among the far left
and the far right young adults,

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:28.773
rather than uniformly increased
across all young adults.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:31.013
And finally,

00:15:31.013 --> 00:15:33.390
we expected that the prime about Israel

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:35.340
should increase antisemitic attitudes

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:37.830
specifically on the ideological left,

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:41.400
since that's where we expect
antisemitic attitudes,

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:45.723
if they exist, to be intertwined
with anti-Israel attitudes.

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.623
So, what did we find?

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:53.160
Here we have charts showing agreement

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:57.300
with each of the three overt
antisemitism questions.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:15:59.400
The X axis on each chart

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:02.580
is going from very liberal
to very conservative,

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.070
and we have the percentage
of respondents in each bucket

00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:07.653
who agreed with the
statement on the Y axis.

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:12.120
The black dots here show
agreement among younger adults.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:15.360
Again, those are people aged 18 to 30.

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:18.570
And the gray squares show
agreement among older adults,

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:19.743
or those over 30.

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:22.260
So right off the bat,

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:23.490
we can see that there's no evidence

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.350
of horseshoe theory here.

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:29.190
For all three questions among
younger and older adults,

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:30.660
respondents on the far left

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:33.090
have some of the lowest rates of agreement

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:36.300
with the overt antisemitic statements.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.310
So instead of a horseshoe pattern,

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:41.190
which we would expect to
look something like this,

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.770
we see that agreement with
antisemitic statements

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:47.703
broadly increases, as we move
from liberal to conservative.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:50.190
But we can also see

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:53.100
that there's a more complicated
relationship with age here

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:56.010
that seems to be modified by ideology.

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:59.100
There isn't much difference on the left

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:03.363
between younger and older
adults, here, here, and here.

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:06.600
But there's a strong
difference on the right,

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:09.210
and we particularly see
this in the boycott,

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.280
and the too much power measure,

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:13.960
and particularly among the
most conservative adults,

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:18.333
there are these huge 40-point gaps here.

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:24.000
So overall, this means that younger adults

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:25.890
do have slightly higher agreement,

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:28.530
especially with the
boycott and power measures,

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:29.730
but that's solely driven

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:32.460
by this really large increase over here

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:36.420
among the young far right,
and it's not at all driven

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:39.663
by any increases among the young far left.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:44.010
So in all, we don't see
a horseshoe pattern,

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:47.040
and instead we see much higher
agreement on the far right,

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:48.483
particularly on far right.

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:55.050
So moving on to looking at the
prime, which as a reminder,

00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:59.370
told respondents first
that 95% of American Jews

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:01.203
have a positive view of Israel.

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:05.070
So this chart shows our
full sample with all adults

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:06.360
just for simplicity

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:08.883
because the results
here don't vary by age.

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:13.290
Here again, we have
ideology on the X axis,

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:15.990
moving from very liberal
to very conservative,

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:19.920
and agreement with each
statement on the Y axis.

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:23.070
So this time, the black dots
represent agreement levels

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:24.993
for respondents who saw the prime.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:30.000
Whereas the gray squares
represent agreement levels

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.683
for respondents who did not.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:34.140
And we expected that the prime

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:38.490
would increase antisemitic
sentiments on the left,

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:42.210
which probably has more
anti-Israel sentiments as well.

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:45.300
However, we don't really
see that that's the case.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.190
We get null and/or small effects

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:49.290
almost across the board here.

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:53.520
We really do not see much
impact for the prime.

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:56.700
The only place we really
do see substantial effects

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:59.430
is right here, among moderates,

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:02.310
when it comes to agreeing specifically

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:06.450
that Jews are more loyal to
Israel than they are to America.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:09.993
You see about a 20-point
increase here for moderates.

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:15.000
Now, the prime not working
doesn't necessarily mean

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:18.390
that the left doesn't hold
negative views of Israel.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:21.090
It could be instead that
it's common knowledge

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:23.700
that Jews hold favorable
views towards Israel

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:25.590
for a lot of our respondents.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:27.990
And thus they didn't
gain any new information

00:19:27.990 --> 00:19:28.833
from the prime.

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:33.420
And perhaps then, the
prime worked on moderates,

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:35.580
specifically for the loyalty measure,

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:38.490
because some research
has found that moderates

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:41.220
may in general know less about politics

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:43.290
and/or be less set in their beliefs.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.330
So they might be more moved by the prime,

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:47.250
specifically for the relevant statement

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:48.783
about Israel loyalty.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:51.630
Another option could be

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:53.730
that the prime just wasn't strong enough

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:57.240
to counteract social
desirability bias on the left.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:00.180
So even when prompted with
information about Israel,

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:01.013
they still know

00:20:01.013 --> 00:20:04.530
that they shouldn't express
these sentiments about Jews.

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:06.270
Now, this lingering concern

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:09.660
about social desirability bias on the left

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:14.640
brings us nicely to our
double standard experiments,

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:18.210
which we used to see if
respondents across the spectrum

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:19.620
are treating Jews differently

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:21.573
from how they treat other groups.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.000
So these experiments

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.670
reduce social desirability concerns a bit,

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:28.980
since we're gonna be
asking the same question,

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:31.800
but with different minority
groups substituted in.

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:35.190
So if they have concerns about
expressing these sentiments,

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:37.860
they should feel it a little less

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.770
if it's directly compared
to another group.

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:43.020
So, how do we do this?

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:47.550
Well, again here, we split our
respondents into two groups.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:50.520
We asked one group if they
thought Jewish Americans

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:53.040
who participate in social justice activism

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:55.320
should have to denounce
Israel's discrimination

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:56.880
against non-Jews.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.560
And we asked the second group

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:02.340
if they thought the same
thing, but for Muslim Americans

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:06.003
denouncing Muslim countries'
discrimination of non-Muslims.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:08.670
Now we picked this question

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:10.770
because there have been
anecdotal news stories

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:14.910
about organizations,
particularly on college campuses

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:17.610
or in other social justice spaces,

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:20.040
that have pressured Jewish members

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:22.830
to denounce Israel or denounce Zionism

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:24.420
if they want to participate.

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:26.730
And we really wanted a
real world example here,

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:29.883
that respondents might have
thought about or heard about.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:33.540
- Sorry Laura, just to jump in with

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:36.093
a quick clarifying question.
- Yeah.

00:21:36.093 --> 00:21:36.926
- [Josh] Can you explain a little bit more

00:21:36.926 --> 00:21:38.670
how this double standard experiment

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:40.570
helps reduce social desirability bias?

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:43.290
You're doing this between subjects design,

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:45.210
so folks are still being asked

00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:48.273
either just about Jews
or just about Muslims.

00:21:49.187 --> 00:21:50.020
- Yes.
- So why,

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:50.853
if they're being asked about Jews,

00:21:50.853 --> 00:21:51.810
don't you think there's a chance

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:53.880
they might hide their true opinion?

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:56.130
- So there definitely is still a chance,

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:57.510
but there's also a chance

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:00.720
they would hide it
against Muslims as well.

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:04.170
So I guess it's sort of
evening the playing field

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:06.840
a little bit about
social desirability bias,

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:10.110
because we can ask
essentially the same question

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:11.610
about two different groups.

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:14.580
So we might think that
social desirability bias

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:18.423
is gonna come into play
for both Jews and Muslims.

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:20.972
- [Josh] Got it, so the assumption is

00:22:20.972 --> 00:22:22.620
there's a standard baseline level

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:25.292
of social desirability
bias against both groups,

00:22:25.292 --> 00:22:26.125
and we're going to partial that out

00:22:26.125 --> 00:22:28.260
by looking at the difference
between the Jews and Muslims.

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:29.260
- Exactly, yeah.

00:22:29.260 --> 00:22:31.230
- [Josh] Yeah, seems plausible.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:32.550
It'd be interesting if you
had data to check that,

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:34.650
but I'll let you keep going.

00:22:34.650 --> 00:22:35.643
- Thanks, yeah.

00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:40.260
So this chart here now shows results,

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:42.600
with the percentage of respondents,

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:45.900
both from the full sample
and just young adults

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:48.240
who agreed with either statement.

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.850
The black squares here
show the respondents

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:53.400
who said that Jews should denounce Israel,

00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:55.950
whereas the gray circles show respondents

00:22:55.950 --> 00:23:00.360
who said that Muslims should
denounce Muslim countries.

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:01.500
We expected to see

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:04.200
an anti-Jewish double
standard on the left,

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.753
and an anti-Muslim double
standard on the right.

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:12.480
So let's just look at the first
half of this chart for now,

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:14.013
the all adults chart.

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:18.000
We see this is the case,

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.340
but we see it to different magnitudes.

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:22.290
We see this double standard on the left,

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:24.873
we see this anti-Jewish
double standard on the left,

00:23:24.873 --> 00:23:26.040
with the far left

00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:29.850
being about 15 to 20 percentage
points more likely to say

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:31.890
that Jews should denounce Israel.

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:34.083
But, looking at the far right,

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:36.540
we see that the far right

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:39.240
is about 40 percentage
points more likely to say

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:42.093
that Muslims should denounce
Muslim discrimination.

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:48.390
And now looking across
the ideological spectrum,

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:52.830
just in this chart, we do see
that those on the far left

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:54.420
are more likely overall

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:56.550
to say that Jews should
have to denounce Israel

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:58.200
than those on the right.

00:23:58.200 --> 00:23:59.190
For those on the left,

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:01.860
again, it's around a little less than 50%.

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:05.940
And on the right, it's
down here around 20, 25%.

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:06.773
So this suggests

00:24:06.773 --> 00:24:09.690
that there may be some
forms of antisemitism,

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:11.760
such as the one here about holding Jews

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.850
to a certain collective standard,

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:18.003
that may be more prominent on
the left than on the right.

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:24.300
But, if we look at just young
adults in the right panel,

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:27.150
we intriguingly see something different.

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:29.610
So here, looking at
respondents' agreements

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.860
that Jews should denounce Israel,

00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:36.270
we see that both the young far
left and the young far right

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:39.720
are much more likely to
agree with this denouncement

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:40.623
than the center.

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:45.690
So unlike any of our previous findings,

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:48.873
here we do actually see
this U-shaped pattern,

00:24:50.190 --> 00:24:52.500
indicating that there is
both a higher standard

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.140
for Jews on the left and the right.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.810
And this is our only indication so far

00:24:57.810 --> 00:25:00.393
of any pattern that would
match horseshoe theory.

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:03.810
We also see that the results for Muslims

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:07.170
remain relatively similar
as for older adults,

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:11.670
generally increases with
ideology for both cases.

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:13.290
And this suggests that something unique

00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:17.730
might be going on here with
young adults and antisemitism.

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:22.000
But, again, there's actually
relatively little change

00:25:24.683 --> 00:25:26.400
between all adults on the far left,

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:28.500
and young adults on the far left.

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:30.630
The new U-shaped pattern

00:25:30.630 --> 00:25:34.500
is coming from young far right respondents

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:37.410
being about 15 percentage
points more likely

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:39.720
to say that Jews should denounce Israel,

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:42.333
than the older far right respondents were.

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:46.260
So while there is some hint, then,

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:47.880
that the far left is exhibiting

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:50.070
a double standard towards Jews,

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:52.410
that doesn't seem to vary by age.

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:54.510
The young far right, instead,

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:56.880
seems to have become much
more critical of Jews

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:59.940
than older far right adults,

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.880
which both lines up with
and provides some nuance

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.250
to our prior findings of antisemitism

00:26:05.250 --> 00:26:07.653
being the strongest among
the young far right.

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:14.010
So we did a second double
standard experiment as well,

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:15.150
to make sure that our answers

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:19.140
weren't solely being driven
by views on Muslim Americans.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:22.980
So here, we used Catholic
Americans and Indian Americans

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.630
as a comparison group.

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:27.150
Catholics, another
religious group that have

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:30.150
an explicitly religious
country affiliated with them,

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:32.670
in the Vatican, and
they've also been accused

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:35.100
of holding dual loyalty to the Vatican.

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:36.690
We use Indian Americans here

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:40.890
as another group associated
with a foreign ally, India.

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:42.600
And Indian Americans also hold

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:45.870
relatively high socioeconomic
status in the US

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:47.373
for a minority group.

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:50.040
So this double standard experiment

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:53.190
actually had a few questions,
but I'm just gonna show one.

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:54.960
And again, the results are similar

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:56.823
across the different questions.

00:26:57.900 --> 00:27:00.840
So we again randomized our
sample into three groups,

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:02.070
and we asked each one

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:06.000
if they thought either Jewish,
Indian, or Catholic Americans

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:07.350
should be held responsible

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:11.730
for Israel, India or
the Vatican's actions.

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:13.230
And here we hypothesized

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:16.050
that both the political
left and the political right

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:18.150
would be more willing
to hold Jewish Americans

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:21.060
responsible for Israel's actions.

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:24.030
This might be the case
due to stereotypes of Jews

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:26.970
as being more powerful
than other minority groups,

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:29.940
but we thought this also might
be present on the far left

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:33.570
due to discourse, again,
around Palestinian activism,

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:36.090
the role Jewish Americans should play,

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:38.583
and general criticism of Israel.

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:41.910
- But looking at--
- Real quick, Laura.

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:42.743
- Yes?
- We just have

00:27:42.743 --> 00:27:44.430
a clarifying question from Michelle--

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:45.480
- Yeah.
- Can you talk a little bit

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:48.990
about the X axis of the
liberal, mod, conservative?

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:51.030
So in particular, Michelle wanted to know,

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:52.650
did these subjects identify themselves

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:54.420
as far right or far left?

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:57.480
- Yeah, so here again,
these axes are going from,

00:27:57.480 --> 00:28:02.010
we ask people, do you identify
as very liberal, liberal,

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:06.090
somewhat liberal, moderate,
somewhat conservative,

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:07.980
conservative, and very conservative.

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:10.980
So we have very liberal here
and very conservative here.

00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:13.800
Again, we did rerun all of this analysis

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:16.473
with specifically more extreme labels,

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:21.300
using leftist, socialist, progressive,

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:24.513
alt-right, Christian
conservative, things like that.

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:27.570
And the results are essentially the same.

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:29.220
But I'm using ideology here

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:33.690
because it's easier to see
a nice trend across ideology

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:36.393
from a more continuous measure.

00:28:37.513 --> 00:28:39.990
- [Josh] And we also have
a question from Cheryl

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:41.820
on this measure of,

00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:45.000
isn't held accountable very
different from should condemn?

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:46.740
So you could talk about maybe
some of the different measures

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:47.670
you looked at.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:49.773
- Yeah, definitely, so held accountable,

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:54.720
we wanted to use these
different measures here,

00:28:54.720 --> 00:28:58.740
because we didn't want to just
have one specific measure.

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:01.230
So our held accountable measure

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:04.920
is slightly different from
our should condemn measure.

00:29:04.920 --> 00:29:06.900
We have another measure,

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:08.763
which is whether or not these groups,

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:14.940
whether Jewish, Indian,
and Catholic Americans

00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:19.320
should do more to make
Israel, India, and the Vatican

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:21.480
sort of better countries

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:25.980
or, yeah, better actors, essentially.

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:28.020
And yeah, you're right that these are

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:30.000
potentially picking up
on different things.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.190
So we wanted to change them a little bit

00:29:32.190 --> 00:29:33.450
just to make sure, again,

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:36.330
that the specific wording
in any of our measures

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:39.600
wasn't accidentally picking up on a trend

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:41.493
and giving us spurious results.

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:48.450
So, looking at our results here,

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:49.380
we can see once again

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:51.900
that we don't have this horseshoe pattern.

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:55.650
Among all adults on the left,

00:29:55.650 --> 00:29:58.440
and among younger adults on the right,

00:29:58.440 --> 00:29:59.910
there's no horseshoe pattern,

00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:02.190
we don't see any of this U-shaped.

00:30:02.190 --> 00:30:04.720
In both cases, adults on the far left

00:30:06.544 --> 00:30:09.450
are the least likely to
hold Jews accountable.

00:30:09.450 --> 00:30:11.100
And there doesn't even seem to really be

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:13.263
an anti-Jewish double standard here.

00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:17.070
We actually find an
anti-Catholic double standard,

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:19.923
which is interesting, but
not the focus of this talk.

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:22.830
The far right also doesn't have

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:24.780
an anti-Jewish double standard.

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:26.520
In both cases, they're the least likely

00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:28.800
to say that Jews should
be held accountable.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:31.200
But, they're still more likely overall

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:34.080
to say that Jews should be
held accountable for Israel

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:36.753
than people on the far left.

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:41.430
And comparing all adults
to younger adults,

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:44.310
we again see that this age effect scales

00:30:44.310 --> 00:30:47.880
as we move ideologically
from liberal to conservative.

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:49.960
So there's essentially no change

00:30:52.765 --> 00:30:56.610
between younger and
older adults on the left.

00:30:56.610 --> 00:31:00.660
But on the far right, we see
that younger far right adults

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:02.940
are about 20 percentage points more likely

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:05.550
to hold Jews responsible for Israel.

00:31:05.550 --> 00:31:08.790
Which, again, suggests
that something unique

00:31:08.790 --> 00:31:13.773
is going on here with young far
right adults' views on Jews.

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:17.430
So, tying this all together,

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:19.680
what did we learn here
about the ideological

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:22.290
and age patterns of antisemitism?

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:25.290
Well, we first learned that
there isn't much evidence

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:28.080
for a broad horseshoe
pattern of antisemitism.

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:30.510
Even when we do see a U-shaped graph,

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:33.783
antisemitism generally remains
much higher on the right.

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:39.870
And so, we find that
antisemitic attitudes generally

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:41.220
are much higher on the far right

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:44.160
than they are on the far left.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:45.630
Looking by age.

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:46.920
We do see that young adults

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:51.150
express more antisemitic
attitudes than older ones do.

00:31:51.150 --> 00:31:53.070
But this is almost entirely

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:55.923
driven by a large increase
on the young right.

00:31:56.880 --> 00:31:58.770
Now we do find a few

00:31:58.770 --> 00:32:01.590
anti-Jewish double standards on the left,

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:03.630
including a few more
subtle ones in the paper

00:32:03.630 --> 00:32:04.950
that I didn't include here,

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:07.020
because they're along the same lines

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:09.240
as the Jewish-Muslim double standard.

00:32:09.240 --> 00:32:11.160
But, in each case,

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:15.120
they're overshadowed by much
stronger anti-Jewish views,

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:19.230
especially on the young
right, for the same questions.

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:21.690
So, I'd say the one-sentence takeaway

00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:24.570
from this talk and this paper
is that horseshoe theory

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:28.560
does not seem to accurately
describe antisemitism in America

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:32.220
since we find that the epicenter

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:35.163
is fairly clearly among
the young far right.

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:37.290
But before I end,

00:32:37.290 --> 00:32:40.230
I do wanna quickly talk
about our two papers,

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:42.210
which shed light on interesting aspects

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:44.043
outside of horseshoe theory here.

00:32:45.390 --> 00:32:47.190
So our second paper in this project

00:32:47.190 --> 00:32:51.030
looks at antisemitic attitudes
among racial minorities,

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:53.760
specifically Black and Hispanic Americans.

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:55.620
The prior work has long shown

00:32:55.620 --> 00:32:59.250
that these groups do tend to
hold more antisemitic attitudes

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:00.930
than white Americans,

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:03.000
and we find that this is true as well,

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:05.523
particularly among young adults.

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:08.280
Somewhat concerningly,

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:10.380
we find that Black and
Hispanic respondents

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:13.740
agree with these overt
antisemitic statements

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:15.420
at around the same rate

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:18.843
as white alt right respondents do.

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:22.290
So, with this finding,

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:25.110
we then examine whether or not our results

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:28.170
match any previously proposed theories

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:30.480
for minority antisemitism.

00:33:30.480 --> 00:33:35.070
These range from greater
Palestinian solidarity,

00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:39.180
to minority groups perhaps
conflating Jews with white people

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:42.000
and actually expressing anti-whiteness,

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:46.830
to geographic conflict or
general group competition,

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:48.840
among many others.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:50.850
So using other questions from our surveys

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:52.650
to test these theories,

00:33:52.650 --> 00:33:55.320
we find that actually none
of these common theories

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:59.280
explain the differences that
we find among our respondents.

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:00.113
And this suggests

00:34:00.113 --> 00:34:02.760
that we really need a
new wave of theorizing

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:04.200
and a lot more research

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:07.710
into the intersection between
race and antisemitism,

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:10.293
to better figure out what's going on here.

00:34:12.180 --> 00:34:14.880
Our third paper is a little different,

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:19.880
and it looks at the young
left's views on Israel.

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:22.590
So underlying a lot of the commentary

00:34:22.590 --> 00:34:24.600
on left wing antisemitism,

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:26.400
is the idea that the far left,

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:30.720
particularly the young far
left, really dislikes Israel.

00:34:30.720 --> 00:34:31.710
But the extent to which

00:34:31.710 --> 00:34:34.830
that's actually empirically
true was unclear.

00:34:34.830 --> 00:34:36.993
So, we decided to find out.

00:34:37.980 --> 00:34:39.960
So using a few more experiments,

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:42.780
and asking about specific countries,

00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:45.210
we find that the young
left does seem to have

00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:49.080
this persistently negative
view towards Israel.

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:53.070
They view Israel more negatively
than older liberals do,

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:55.323
or other young adults do.

00:34:56.160 --> 00:35:00.300
And young far left adults also
view Israel more negatively

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:01.950
than they view other countries

00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:03.993
allied with the United States.

00:35:05.310 --> 00:35:07.770
So checking two plausible explanations,

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:10.680
we find that this view
doesn't seem to be explained

00:35:10.680 --> 00:35:12.870
by an issue prioritization

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:16.380
of the Israel-Palestine
conflict among the left.

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:18.810
And we also find that
their negative attitudes

00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:22.290
extend beyond criticisms
of Israel's government,

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:25.020
and seem to spill over
into criticism of things

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:28.173
like Israel's culture,
languages and religion.

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:30.600
So these findings

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:33.540
also don't fit neatly
with existing theories,

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:34.650
or what we'd expect

00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:37.860
from partisan polarized
views of foreign countries.

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:41.940
So, we again argue that more
research is really needed here,

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:45.090
to grapple seriously with what's going on,

00:35:45.090 --> 00:35:48.783
with views of both Jews and Israel.

00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:53.700
And that's all I have here.

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:54.780
I wanted to mention

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:57.930
that I have a few new
projects I'm starting on

00:35:57.930 --> 00:35:59.970
that are somewhat related.

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:01.800
One is on the connection

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:05.520
between antisemitism
and conspiracy theories,

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:09.930
and another is on how the
conditional whiteness of Jews

00:36:09.930 --> 00:36:11.910
and Jews' relatively high status

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:14.460
affects perceptions of antisemitism.

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:17.670
But I'd love to hear from
everyone here, any thoughts

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:20.670
on what people found the
most compelling about this,

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:23.370
or best directions to go in from here

00:36:23.370 --> 00:36:25.530
with this specific avenue of research.

00:36:25.530 --> 00:36:27.900
As well, of course, as any questions

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:32.550
or really any comments
about any part of this.

00:36:32.550 --> 00:36:33.723
So, thanks.

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:35.700
- Thank you so much Laura.

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:37.950
Super, super interesting work.

00:36:37.950 --> 00:36:39.480
So if folks have any questions,

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:43.050
please put those in the Q and
A and I will moderate them.

00:36:43.050 --> 00:36:45.450
We already have a few questions there now.

00:36:45.450 --> 00:36:49.207
So first question is "How do
we know that these results

00:36:49.207 --> 00:36:51.447
"are not due to random
variability in the sample?

00:36:51.447 --> 00:36:53.197
"In other words, were
statistical tests used

00:36:53.197 --> 00:36:55.770
"to determine if these results
are actually significant."

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:56.880
So can you talk a little bit

00:36:56.880 --> 00:36:58.860
about those conference
intervals on your graphs?

00:36:58.860 --> 00:36:59.693
- Yeah, definitely.

00:36:59.693 --> 00:37:02.490
So all the confidence intervals,

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:04.200
I guess I should share my screen again,

00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:06.000
instead of having stopped shared it.

00:37:08.340 --> 00:37:11.400
Yeah, so all of the confidence intervals

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:12.600
shown on our graphs here,

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:15.810
these are 95% confidence intervals.

00:37:15.810 --> 00:37:18.390
So we use standard confidence intervals

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:20.220
to test that actually,

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:23.760
these are statistically significant gaps,

00:37:23.760 --> 00:37:26.340
and based on, we have a
pretty large sample size,

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:27.690
so we can be pretty sure

00:37:27.690 --> 00:37:30.330
that these are statistically significant

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:33.573
and we're actually picking
up on real differences,

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:36.510
instead of random variability.

00:37:36.510 --> 00:37:39.720
And again, having sort of this large,

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:43.560
fairly spread out sample
across the ideological spectrum

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:44.823
really helps us here.

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:47.123
- Thanks.

00:37:47.123 --> 00:37:48.997
Another question from David Caslow.

00:37:48.997 --> 00:37:51.127
"What beliefs are connected
with antisemitism?

00:37:51.127 --> 00:37:53.707
"Specifically is the belief in zero sum,

00:37:53.707 --> 00:37:55.237
"that there is only a fixed amount

00:37:55.237 --> 00:37:56.917
"of happiness, success, money,

00:37:56.917 --> 00:37:59.077
"and that an increase in
this amount in one group

00:37:59.077 --> 00:38:01.267
"decreases this amount in another group?

00:38:01.267 --> 00:38:02.797
"Is this idea of zero sum of belief

00:38:02.797 --> 00:38:04.147
"connected with the antisemitism

00:38:04.147 --> 00:38:06.367
"and something you studied
in either this paper

00:38:06.367 --> 00:38:08.100
"or other work you have ongoing?"

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:10.530
- Yeah, so that comes up.

00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:12.960
That's definitely a proposed theory,

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:15.840
that comes up specifically
in a lot of the theories

00:38:15.840 --> 00:38:18.030
about racial minorities.

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:21.060
Sort of one of the new theories there

00:38:21.060 --> 00:38:25.560
is that there's sort of zero
sum either material resources,

00:38:25.560 --> 00:38:30.000
or even zero sum sympathy
that society awards

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.760
to different minority
groups who have suffered.

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:36.480
And so some of the antisemitic sentiment

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.040
we could be picking up on

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:41.460
might be because Jews are perceived

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:46.460
as both sort of the victims
of antisemitism, but, again,

00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:50.550
they're well off in
America socioeconomically.

00:38:50.550 --> 00:38:54.337
So this does create sort
of this odd tension of,

00:38:54.337 --> 00:38:55.957
"Well, they're being
discriminated against,

00:38:55.957 --> 00:38:59.130
"but they're doing better
than other minority groups."

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:02.580
So that's something I'm really
interested in looking at

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:07.050
with my next project about
views of privilege and status

00:39:07.050 --> 00:39:10.320
and how that affects
perceptions of antisemitism,

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:13.770
does that lead people to be
less sympathetic towards Jews

00:39:13.770 --> 00:39:18.510
or to express more antisemitic
sentiments in general?

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:20.160
- Question from Eran Rubenstein.

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:24.990
So have you looked at any sort
of geographical differences,

00:39:24.990 --> 00:39:28.410
specifically urban
versus non-urban pattern

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:29.400
in these results?

00:39:29.400 --> 00:39:32.400
And I'll add on top of that
is, have you looked at,

00:39:32.400 --> 00:39:33.960
especially among the young people,

00:39:33.960 --> 00:39:36.270
college educated versus
not college educated?

00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:38.460
- Yep, so we look at both of these things.

00:39:38.460 --> 00:39:41.790
So I didn't include any of
our regression tables here,

00:39:41.790 --> 00:39:44.370
because I think it's
generally very overwhelming

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:48.420
when someone puts up a gigantic
regression table in a slide.

00:39:48.420 --> 00:39:52.740
But all of these results here
that I'm showing on graphs,

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:55.470
we do regression analysis as well,

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:57.720
and we find the same
results for all of them.

00:39:57.720 --> 00:39:58.650
But in those cases,

00:39:58.650 --> 00:40:01.980
we're able to control
for college education,

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:04.500
and geography in particular.

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:07.260
So we look at both of those things.

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:08.760
And specifically in our paper

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:12.570
about racial minority antisemitism,

00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:15.870
those are two common confounder
theories that are proposed,

00:40:15.870 --> 00:40:16.980
for why racial groups

00:40:16.980 --> 00:40:19.920
might have higher rates of antisemitism.

00:40:19.920 --> 00:40:23.250
And we basically dismiss both of those.

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:25.710
So yes, we have information about college,

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:27.420
which we've used to confirm.

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:30.510
For geography, we only
have state-level data

00:40:30.510 --> 00:40:32.280
unfortunately, from YouGov.

00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:35.370
So we can't do anything as direct

00:40:35.370 --> 00:40:37.800
as Ryan Enos does in his work,

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:39.840
when he can directly sort of look at

00:40:39.840 --> 00:40:44.760
how neighborhoods and
contact increases prejudice.

00:40:44.760 --> 00:40:47.820
But, there are actually a lot of states

00:40:47.820 --> 00:40:49.980
where there are essentially no Jews.

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:54.480
So we do compare people who
respond from those states,

00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:57.480
assuming they have essentially
no contact with Jews,

00:40:57.480 --> 00:40:59.220
with people responding from states

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:01.740
that have a much higher Jewish population,

00:41:01.740 --> 00:41:04.620
like New York, California, Florida,

00:41:04.620 --> 00:41:07.370
and we don't see...

00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:11.520
All of these things still persist,

00:41:11.520 --> 00:41:14.820
all of our results still
persist and are robust to that.

00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:17.947
- In a related question
from Michelle Edgar, so,

00:41:17.947 --> 00:41:20.917
"Some states require holocaust education

00:41:20.917 --> 00:41:23.557
"as as part of the K through 12 schooling

00:41:23.557 --> 00:41:25.267
"and other states do not.

00:41:25.267 --> 00:41:27.607
"Have you looked at whether
there are any differences

00:41:27.607 --> 00:41:28.780
"between states that have

00:41:28.780 --> 00:41:30.547
"that Holocaust education requirement

00:41:30.547 --> 00:41:32.010
"and states that don't?"

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:33.600
- Yeah, we haven't looked at that,

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:36.000
but that's actually a great idea,

00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:41.000
and something that I'm curious
about and might fit into

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:44.370
maybe the project about
perceptions of Jews,

00:41:44.370 --> 00:41:48.450
is along those lines, but it's about,

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:51.090
when we ask people questions about Jews,

00:41:51.090 --> 00:41:54.000
presumably a large
portion of our respondents

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:56.040
don't know any Jewish people.

00:41:56.040 --> 00:41:57.690
So what are they thinking about?

00:41:57.690 --> 00:42:01.147
There's probably some
respondents who are like,

00:42:01.147 --> 00:42:03.487
"Oh, my friends are Jewish,

00:42:03.487 --> 00:42:06.183
"and they're completely normal people.

00:42:08.098 --> 00:42:09.270
"I like them."

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:11.160
But if you don't know Jews,

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:12.120
what are you thinking of

00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:13.800
when you think of a Jewish American?

00:42:13.800 --> 00:42:18.750
Are you thinking of some
stereotype of a Hasidic Jew

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:21.210
living an insular community?

00:42:21.210 --> 00:42:24.330
Because that's often how,
especially observant Jews,

00:42:24.330 --> 00:42:27.030
are portrayed in media and in the news.

00:42:27.030 --> 00:42:32.030
Or are you thinking of a random Reform Jew

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:35.730
who lives in your town and
lives in your community?

00:42:35.730 --> 00:42:37.770
So that's something that I
think is really interesting

00:42:37.770 --> 00:42:39.540
and I would love to look at more,

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:41.850
because I'm very interested in how

00:42:41.850 --> 00:42:45.180
what people know about Judaism
and how all these things

00:42:45.180 --> 00:42:47.670
lead to different views on Jews

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:50.910
and different parts of antisemitism.

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:51.743
- And I guess following up

00:42:51.743 --> 00:42:53.633
on that question about stereotypes, so,

00:42:53.633 --> 00:42:55.920
I think Susan Fiske has done some work

00:42:55.920 --> 00:42:58.770
on stereotypes towards Jews,
and if I remember correctly,

00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:01.740
she finds that most Americans
hold these stereotypes

00:43:01.740 --> 00:43:03.630
where Jews are viewed
as as high competence

00:43:03.630 --> 00:43:06.663
but maybe low on warmth or viewed coolly.

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:10.080
Assuming that my recollection is correct,

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:13.800
does she look at all across
either age or ideology,

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:16.740
and does she see any sort of difference

00:43:16.740 --> 00:43:18.240
along the patterns that
you're finding here,

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:19.440
or have you looked at--
- Yeah.

00:43:19.440 --> 00:43:22.080
- These types of like Susan
Fiske stereotypes questions

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:23.280
in your own work?

00:43:23.280 --> 00:43:26.970
- Yeah, I'm not sure off the
top of my head what she finds,

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:27.803
but that's something

00:43:27.803 --> 00:43:32.790
that I have been thinking
about a lot here.

00:43:32.790 --> 00:43:37.080
I know there's also work on
how Asian Americans are viewed,

00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:39.840
and sort of the idea of
racial triangulation,

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:41.640
which has been going back, I don't know,

00:43:41.640 --> 00:43:43.650
a decade, two decades.

00:43:43.650 --> 00:43:45.870
And the idea that minority groups

00:43:45.870 --> 00:43:49.590
aren't just viewed on a certain
spectrum, as we would say,

00:43:49.590 --> 00:43:52.020
from bad to good.

00:43:52.020 --> 00:43:53.280
But there's a sense of, yeah,

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:56.880
are they viewed coolly or
warmly, or in another sense,

00:43:56.880 --> 00:44:00.860
are they viewed as Americans

00:44:00.860 --> 00:44:03.120
or are they viewed as permanent outsiders?

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:04.380
And then also are they viewed

00:44:04.380 --> 00:44:08.610
as competent to incompetent, essentially?

00:44:08.610 --> 00:44:11.160
And so I think there
definitely is this push,

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:13.020
within a lot of the prejudice literature,

00:44:13.020 --> 00:44:16.560
to think of things not
just on a single spectrum,

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:18.390
and that's something I would definitely

00:44:18.390 --> 00:44:20.940
want to expand this work out to,

00:44:20.940 --> 00:44:24.453
'cause I think it's an
obvious place to go from here.

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:27.270
- So we have a few questions from folks

00:44:27.270 --> 00:44:29.970
on what I'll call the why question.

00:44:29.970 --> 00:44:34.970
Why is it that the young left
uniquely dislikes Israel?

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:37.650
Have you thought about this,

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:39.120
have you started to investigate it,

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:43.740
what is the role of of social
media, or media more broadly,

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:45.360
and opinion leadership,

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:47.130
and a different crop of elected officials

00:44:47.130 --> 00:44:49.350
coming from the left, and
their views towards Israel?

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:51.600
Just, yeah, what have you thought about

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:53.610
when it comes to this question of why,

00:44:53.610 --> 00:44:55.890
have you started to answer
this question, if not,

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:58.440
how do you think you would
go about answering this?

00:44:58.440 --> 00:45:00.540
- Yeah, great question.

00:45:00.540 --> 00:45:05.460
I have definitely thought
a lot about why this is.

00:45:05.460 --> 00:45:07.560
The question of media, I think.

00:45:07.560 --> 00:45:12.560
Okay, so, we can say
one potential reason is,

00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:16.620
that the far left is
actually just antisemitic,

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:19.290
and they're channeling all of those views

00:45:19.290 --> 00:45:23.250
into a more socially
acceptable sort of output,

00:45:23.250 --> 00:45:25.380
which would be criticism of Israel.

00:45:25.380 --> 00:45:27.510
That is sort of one extreme,

00:45:27.510 --> 00:45:29.610
that may or may not be the case.

00:45:29.610 --> 00:45:32.310
But there's lots of other potential ideas

00:45:32.310 --> 00:45:34.110
that we need to check first.

00:45:34.110 --> 00:45:36.210
The biggest one is probably this question

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:39.210
of media and exposure.

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:43.440
So it might be that people on the far left

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:46.740
really just see lots
more news about Israel,

00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:49.710
they see a lot more news
about Israel and Palestine.

00:45:49.710 --> 00:45:53.040
Either because news media covers it more

00:45:53.040 --> 00:45:57.450
than conflicts in other
countries, which is probably true.

00:45:57.450 --> 00:46:00.150
Or that people on social media,

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:01.920
these opinion leaders, as you talk about,

00:46:01.920 --> 00:46:05.040
are posting more about
Israel and Palestine.

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:08.700
We see, especially, the
squad, you know, AOC,

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:12.120
Rashida Tlaib, Ilhan
Omar, Ayanna Pressley,

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:14.570
they talk about Israel
and Palestine quite a bit.

00:46:15.510 --> 00:46:17.730
So it could be that the far left

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:20.550
holds these uniquely
negative views on Israel,

00:46:20.550 --> 00:46:23.230
just because they know
more and they are exposed

00:46:25.382 --> 00:46:27.780
to more negative criticism of Israel,

00:46:27.780 --> 00:46:31.020
and also just exposed in general
to what's going on there.

00:46:31.020 --> 00:46:33.090
Because there's so much
going on in the world,

00:46:33.090 --> 00:46:36.690
and we know people can't
really hold real opinions

00:46:36.690 --> 00:46:40.830
about what's going on in
every single country at once.

00:46:40.830 --> 00:46:45.090
So, I would say those are
sort of the two extremes

00:46:45.090 --> 00:46:47.370
as to what could be going
on with the far left.

00:46:47.370 --> 00:46:50.733
And it could be anywhere
along that spectrum.

00:46:51.690 --> 00:46:54.663
I haven't yet started
digging into why that is,

00:46:56.550 --> 00:46:58.080
in part because it's a thorny issue,

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:00.720
it's hard to really get at the core

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:04.080
of why do people feel
this way about Israel?

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:07.320
Because again, with social
desirability bias coming in,

00:47:07.320 --> 00:47:10.260
if you ask someone who
really is antisemitic,

00:47:10.260 --> 00:47:13.020
who holds negative views of Israel,

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:14.190
why do you feel this way?

00:47:14.190 --> 00:47:15.120
They're probably gonna come up

00:47:15.120 --> 00:47:20.120
with a reasonable justification
that's not "I hate Jews."

00:47:20.370 --> 00:47:24.660
So, it's a tricky thing
to get at, but I think,

00:47:24.660 --> 00:47:27.540
actually going back to what
we were just talking about,

00:47:27.540 --> 00:47:31.680
exploring what people
actually know about Israel,

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:34.740
and what they know about the
conflict, and things like this,

00:47:34.740 --> 00:47:38.040
could be one way to go about it.

00:47:38.040 --> 00:47:39.890
And I've considered something...

00:47:43.350 --> 00:47:46.380
I talked earlier about
a lot of the parallels

00:47:46.380 --> 00:47:48.870
that are drawn between Black Lives Matter

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:51.630
and the Israel-Palestine conflict.

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:55.170
And something I'm broadly
interested in is whether,

00:47:55.170 --> 00:47:57.120
especially people on the far left,

00:47:57.120 --> 00:47:59.340
not only see those as parallels,

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:02.490
but sort of impute US race relations

00:48:02.490 --> 00:48:04.320
onto Israel and Palestine.

00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:08.130
So do they view Israelis, as
they view Jews in America,

00:48:08.130 --> 00:48:09.870
who are primarily white?

00:48:09.870 --> 00:48:12.930
And do they view Palestinians as,

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:15.037
they're primarily Arabs, so,

00:48:15.037 --> 00:48:17.757
"Okay, Muslims in the United
States are viewed as not white,

00:48:17.757 --> 00:48:19.770
"so I will view them as not white."

00:48:19.770 --> 00:48:21.720
And that's sort of a simple issue

00:48:21.720 --> 00:48:24.330
for the far left to have a stance on.

00:48:24.330 --> 00:48:26.700
Because here in the United States,

00:48:26.700 --> 00:48:29.010
white people they would
view as the oppressors, so,

00:48:29.010 --> 00:48:31.353
they can do the same as
Israel and Palestine.

00:48:32.430 --> 00:48:35.070
Now, we also know that,

00:48:35.070 --> 00:48:38.790
I believe still the
majority of Jews in Israel

00:48:38.790 --> 00:48:41.730
are not Ashkenazi, they're not white,

00:48:41.730 --> 00:48:45.630
they're not coming from
Europe or even America.

00:48:45.630 --> 00:48:48.540
So I'm interested, one
thing I had a thought about,

00:48:48.540 --> 00:48:51.330
is if you prime people with information

00:48:51.330 --> 00:48:55.590
about racial demographics in Israel,

00:48:55.590 --> 00:48:57.600
about Israelis and Palestinians,

00:48:57.600 --> 00:49:01.740
does that change any
views they have on Israel?

00:49:01.740 --> 00:49:04.320
And I haven't really thought
it through further than that,

00:49:04.320 --> 00:49:05.153
but that's something

00:49:05.153 --> 00:49:07.650
I would be really interested to work on,

00:49:07.650 --> 00:49:09.363
and someone should do, if not me.

00:49:10.980 --> 00:49:13.130
- You should do it,
it's a great next step.

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:17.310
And a few questions sort
of from the the other side,

00:49:17.310 --> 00:49:18.143
the far right.

00:49:18.143 --> 00:49:21.990
So, one question is about
just why you think this is,

00:49:21.990 --> 00:49:24.480
there's an age gap on the far right,

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:26.010
between younger and older?

00:49:26.010 --> 00:49:29.310
And a related question
is about religiosity.

00:49:29.310 --> 00:49:30.900
How much of this do you think is driven

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:35.310
by religiously-based antisemitic attitudes

00:49:35.310 --> 00:49:38.520
that might hold both among
right-wing white Christians,

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:41.280
but also among the Black
and Latino population

00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:45.270
that is more religious?
- Yeah, so,

00:49:45.270 --> 00:49:49.320
religiosity is an easier answer, which is,

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:51.030
we actually control for this,

00:49:51.030 --> 00:49:53.610
both in the ideology paper

00:49:53.610 --> 00:49:57.060
and specifically in the
minority antisemitism paper.

00:49:57.060 --> 00:50:01.350
And we do find often higher
rates of antisemitism

00:50:01.350 --> 00:50:04.260
among more religious people.

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:06.840
But, these patterns do persist,

00:50:06.840 --> 00:50:10.080
even just looking at
more religious people,

00:50:10.080 --> 00:50:11.890
still people on the far right

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:15.240
agree more with these
antisemitic statements,

00:50:15.240 --> 00:50:18.240
and the same for breaking
down racial minority groups.

00:50:18.240 --> 00:50:23.240
So, religiosity certainly
plays a role in people's views

00:50:23.790 --> 00:50:25.710
on Jews and antisemitism,

00:50:25.710 --> 00:50:29.373
but it doesn't explain any of
these patterns specifically.

00:50:31.530 --> 00:50:35.070
Going back to why I think young far right

00:50:35.070 --> 00:50:37.680
might be more likely to agree.

00:50:37.680 --> 00:50:38.820
There's a few things.

00:50:38.820 --> 00:50:42.483
One is, well, maybe it's
sort of the same thing.

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:47.613
I talked about the growth of
the alt-right movement online.

00:50:49.050 --> 00:50:53.580
Part of sort of the ethos
of the alt-right movement

00:50:53.580 --> 00:50:56.100
and people on the young
far right in general,

00:50:56.100 --> 00:50:58.350
is this sort of reactionary attitude

00:50:58.350 --> 00:51:01.470
to a lot of so-called
political correctness,

00:51:01.470 --> 00:51:03.480
things like that that are going on.

00:51:03.480 --> 00:51:05.430
And we see people on the young far right

00:51:05.430 --> 00:51:10.430
being more willing to just
express hateful views broadly,

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:12.930
we can think about how
social media trolling

00:51:12.930 --> 00:51:15.000
might have played a role in that.

00:51:15.000 --> 00:51:18.240
So it might be that people
on the young far right,

00:51:18.240 --> 00:51:20.250
it could be that there's actually

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:23.370
a decreased social desirability bias

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:25.890
among young far right adults,

00:51:25.890 --> 00:51:28.830
it could be that there're
being reactionary,

00:51:28.830 --> 00:51:31.410
they think it's good to
share these honest attitudes,

00:51:31.410 --> 00:51:33.067
they're like, "I'm not afraid to say

00:51:33.067 --> 00:51:35.490
"what I actually think about Jews,"

00:51:35.490 --> 00:51:37.620
and perhaps other minority groups.

00:51:37.620 --> 00:51:41.790
But it also could be that because

00:51:41.790 --> 00:51:44.580
lots of these white
nationalist, alt-right movements

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:46.200
are growing online,

00:51:46.200 --> 00:51:49.260
they're particularly
attracting young adults,

00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:51.180
who are overwhelmingly more online,

00:51:51.180 --> 00:51:52.650
who are gonna find these communities,

00:51:52.650 --> 00:51:55.350
they're gonna be on
4chan, things like that.

00:51:55.350 --> 00:51:58.920
And it could be that just
this increased exposure,

00:51:58.920 --> 00:52:02.610
to all of this, is making them express

00:52:02.610 --> 00:52:05.433
more antisemitic attitudes in general.

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:10.323
- And one question from David Nelson is,

00:52:11.550 --> 00:52:14.670
is there a way to separate
out these attitudes from,

00:52:14.670 --> 00:52:18.810
let's call it genuine attitudes
versus peer-based attitudes,

00:52:18.810 --> 00:52:22.230
where in some groups it's the cool thing

00:52:22.230 --> 00:52:23.130
or the in-group thing

00:52:23.130 --> 00:52:25.260
to hold these antisemitic attitudes,

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:26.580
even if the individual expressing them

00:52:26.580 --> 00:52:30.480
does not truly harbor these
antisemitic attitudes.

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:31.530
- Yeah.

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:32.810
- Do you have any thoughts on that,

00:52:32.810 --> 00:52:35.100
and I guess a related question would be,

00:52:35.100 --> 00:52:36.837
is there a relationship
between these attitudes

00:52:36.837 --> 00:52:39.303
and any type of antisemitic behavior?

00:52:40.260 --> 00:52:43.350
- Yeah, both of those are great questions.

00:52:43.350 --> 00:52:46.800
We don't have any way of linking that

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:48.240
to antisemitic behavior,

00:52:48.240 --> 00:52:52.260
and I'm not sure if anyone has
ever asked those questions,

00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:53.670
but that would be

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:56.463
a really interesting
thing to look at as well.

00:52:58.110 --> 00:52:59.310
I think it's a great question,

00:52:59.310 --> 00:53:01.050
and I think partially what it's getting at

00:53:01.050 --> 00:53:06.000
is this idea of expressive
responding on surveys.

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:09.930
Which is sort of a problem, a concern

00:53:09.930 --> 00:53:12.750
that comes up more broadly
in political science.

00:53:12.750 --> 00:53:16.860
Which is, what if people,
when they take a survey,

00:53:16.860 --> 00:53:19.740
what if they're not responding
with their genuine view,

00:53:19.740 --> 00:53:21.270
but they're responding with the view

00:53:21.270 --> 00:53:25.110
they know they should hold
based on some identity?

00:53:25.110 --> 00:53:26.970
And we're often really
concerned about this

00:53:26.970 --> 00:53:28.920
when it comes to partisanship,

00:53:28.920 --> 00:53:30.330
and thinking about polarization.

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:33.450
So we see very polarized views
among the left and the right

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:35.730
on all sorts of policy issues.

00:53:35.730 --> 00:53:39.840
And one question is, well,
if you ask people their views

00:53:39.840 --> 00:53:43.860
on affirmative action, for example,

00:53:43.860 --> 00:53:44.857
are people who are saying,

00:53:44.857 --> 00:53:47.257
"Well I'm a Democrat, so I'm
supposed to support this,

00:53:47.257 --> 00:53:48.750
"so I'll say yes."

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:50.550
Are they saying that when
they fill out a survey

00:53:50.550 --> 00:53:54.510
or are they actually truly in
support of affirmative action?

00:53:54.510 --> 00:53:56.640
It's very hard to
disentangle these things,

00:53:56.640 --> 00:53:59.073
and that could be part
of what's going on here.

00:54:01.230 --> 00:54:06.230
I would say, it's partially, I would lean,

00:54:08.130 --> 00:54:11.010
my gut instinct is that
that's less of a concern

00:54:11.010 --> 00:54:12.750
with our question specifically

00:54:12.750 --> 00:54:16.080
than with a lot of these
more policy-based questions,

00:54:16.080 --> 00:54:19.200
because it's not exactly clear

00:54:19.200 --> 00:54:23.100
that there should be a strict party divide

00:54:23.100 --> 00:54:24.030
on these issues.

00:54:24.030 --> 00:54:25.417
You know, we might think,

00:54:25.417 --> 00:54:29.640
"Oh, white nationalists, they're
gonna dislike Jews less."

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:34.080
But the Republican Party is
also very supportive of Israel.

00:54:34.080 --> 00:54:34.913
And of course,

00:54:34.913 --> 00:54:36.630
that doesn't mean that
they're not antisemitic,

00:54:36.630 --> 00:54:39.483
these two views are sort of separate, but,

00:54:40.380 --> 00:54:42.697
a lot of people on the far
right would probably say,

00:54:42.697 --> 00:54:44.797
"Oh yeah, I am supportive of Jews,

00:54:44.797 --> 00:54:47.460
"I support Israel, I
support all these things."

00:54:47.460 --> 00:54:50.130
So it's not clear exactly

00:54:50.130 --> 00:54:54.150
that there would be such
strong expressive responding.

00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:56.610
But it is something worth exploring

00:54:56.610 --> 00:54:58.863
and looking at more, definitely.

00:54:59.730 --> 00:55:00.870
- Perhaps one way you could get at this

00:55:00.870 --> 00:55:03.990
is about people's
perceptions of group norms,

00:55:03.990 --> 00:55:06.900
so you could ask them, what
percent of your friends

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:07.733
do you think would agree

00:55:07.733 --> 00:55:10.569
with this statement
about double standards?

00:55:10.569 --> 00:55:11.402
And to see if there's a gap

00:55:11.402 --> 00:55:14.640
between those group norm
perceptions and people's positions.

00:55:14.640 --> 00:55:16.623
- Yeah, that's a great idea, thanks.

00:55:19.950 --> 00:55:22.743
- So we are almost at the top of the hour.

00:55:24.720 --> 00:55:27.930
I believe this talk has been recorded

00:55:27.930 --> 00:55:32.930
and will be shared, Inasela, somewhere,

00:55:33.900 --> 00:55:35.370
if you wanna chime in, I meant--

00:55:35.370 --> 00:55:36.389
- Yeah, sure.
- Since if we got

00:55:36.389 --> 00:55:37.440
a question on that.

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:42.440
- [Inasela] Yes, it will be
posted on the YPSA website.

00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:46.743
It's ypsa.yale.edu.

00:55:47.610 --> 00:55:48.903
In a couple of weeks.

00:55:50.280 --> 00:55:51.330
- Great, thanks.

00:55:51.330 --> 00:55:53.460
And Laura, if folks are interested

00:55:53.460 --> 00:55:55.560
in reading these additional papers,

00:55:55.560 --> 00:55:59.013
is there a place where they
can go to access those?

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:05.310
- Probably.
(chuckles)

00:56:05.310 --> 00:56:06.510
I would guess they're probably

00:56:06.510 --> 00:56:09.720
on my co-author Eitan Hirsch's website,

00:56:09.720 --> 00:56:12.180
so you could go there, but also,

00:56:12.180 --> 00:56:16.140
anyone is free to contact
me, reach out on Twitter.

00:56:16.140 --> 00:56:18.420
You can find my email and email me,

00:56:18.420 --> 00:56:20.700
and I'd be happy to share this more

00:56:20.700 --> 00:56:23.133
with anyone who's interested.

00:56:24.578 --> 00:56:25.411
- So folks are interested

00:56:25.411 --> 00:56:28.800
in the other papers that
we got a brief intro to,

00:56:28.800 --> 00:56:30.210
but lots of discussion about,

00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:32.343
please reach out to Laura on those.

00:56:34.410 --> 00:56:36.510
So yeah, thank you all so much,

00:56:36.510 --> 00:56:39.120
